within Pharmacolibrary.Drugs.V_Various.V03A_AllOtherTherapeuticProducts.V03AF02_Dexrazoxane;
model Dexrazoxane_Mody2023v2_PD_r
  "linear exposure-response sweep — Mody_2023_2 :: R (AC16 cell viability)"
  extends Pharmacolibrary.Interfaces.PartialExposureResponseSweep(
    E0 = 1.01,      // [1; % × 0.01]
    Emax = 0, EC50 = 1,      // unused by a line
    exposure_per_s = 1);
  parameter Real slope = 9680   "[1 / meter; µM−1 × 1e+06]";
equation
  response = E0 + slope*exposure;
  annotation(experiment(StartTime = 0, StopTime = 1000, Interval = 2),
    Documentation(info = "<html><body><h4>Dexrazoxane_Mody2023v2_PD_r</h4><p>The steady-state exposure-response curve of Mody H et al., <i>Scientific reports 13(1):3100</i> 2023 (<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-29964-4\">doi:10.1038/s41598-023-29964-4</a>) — AC16 cell viability [%] against dexrazoxane µM — obtained by sweeping the driver instead of simulating a dose. Model family: linear.</p><h4>The parameters, in the paper's terms</h4><pre>  E0    =        101 %        drug-free response (baseline) (R0, Q324)
  slope =    0.00968 µM−1     change in response per unit of exposure (SDEX, Q335)</pre><p>The algebra is <code>response = E0 + slope*exposure</code>; this model only supplies the numbers. The x axis is one µM of dexrazoxane per second of simulated time: there is no state, no dose and no kinetics — plot <code>response</code> against <code>exposure</code> (or <code>time</code>).</p><h4>What it reproduces</h4><pre>  exposure = 0         response 1.01   (drug-free)</pre></body></html>", revisions = "<html><body><ul><li>11/2026 generated by pharmacolibrary-dev export.pharmacolibrary_models from Mody_2023_2: parameters extracted from the paper by the pharmacolibrary-dev pipeline, model built by its engineer, reviewed: curated candidate</li></ul></body></html>"));
end Dexrazoxane_Mody2023v2_PD_r;
